Title: DRY CROSSING
Author: Sam Whitaker
Draft date: June 2026

FADE IN:


EXT. MCCREADY RANCH - DAWN

Cattle low. Smoke from the cookhouse. Another dry month carved into every face.

EXT. DRY RIVER CROSSING - DAY

HOLT MCCREADY's herd stalled at a vanished ford.
HOLT
We move or lose half by sundown.

EXT. RIDGE TRAIL - DAY

Dawson's rock dam visible below, diverting the flow to his south pasture. Jesse spits dust.
JESSE
He always wanted this crossing. This is railroad work, Holt — somebody's paying for a feud.

INT. DAWSON RANCH HOUSE - DAY

WILL DAWSON, 45, and Holt — tense, rifle within reach.
DAWSON
River changed on God's timetable. My north fields finally drink.

EXT. MCCREADY RANCH - NIGHT

Holt's wife, CLARA, sets out food for riders. Worry unspoken.
CLARA
You go to Dawson with words. Come back with words.

EXT. DRY RIVER CROSSING - DUSK

Failed crossing. A steer trapped. Warning shots from the ridge.

EXT. DAWSON RIDGE - DUSK

Holt rides up — alone, hands visible. Dawson's men tense.
HOLT
You didn't fire those shots. Who did?

INT. DAWSON RANCH - NIGHT

Holt and Dawson, fifty now and tired, compare notes — both found dynamite near the bed.
DAWSON
Railroad man paid for a feud. Cheaper than buying us out.

EXT. DRY RIVER CROSSING - NIGHT

Holt finds a fuse line leading to the dam. Fresh footprints — city boots.

EXT. DRY RIVER CROSSING - NIGHT

Holt cuts the fuse. Dawson arrives from the opposite bank — first time without a rifle raised.
HOLT
We tear the dam together. Or we both lose the land to a track line.
DAWSON
God's timetable, huh. Grab a hammer.

FADE OUT.


THE END
